  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 139 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nSaturday, upper level ridging begins to expand north and eastward \nfrom the desert southwest. Meanwhile at the surface, high pressure \nwill shift eastward to near the Iowa/Nebraska by Saturday evening. \nWinds will shift around to the southeast but are not expected to be \nstrong. This should limit any fire weather concerns, even across far \nwestern Nebraska and the Panhandle where afternoon humidity drops \ninto the 20-25% range and highs reach into the lower 90s. \n\nUpper level ridging continues to expand across the area Sunday. This \nwill continue to bring more warming temperatures with highs \nexceeding 90 degrees at all locations. Deepening low pressure across \nWyoming ahead of a broad long wave trough across the northern \nRockies will bring strong, gusty south winds, particularly west of \nHighway 83. Highs in these areas will reach the mid to perhaps upper \n90s with humidity dropping into the 18-25% range. Even though some \nareas have received decent rainfall, overall drought conditions make \nthe environment conducive fire weather concerns. These concerns will \nlikely become elevated to near critical across the western Sandhills \nand the Panhandle Sunday afternoon...due to the combination of gusty \nwinds, very warm temperatures and low humidity.  \n\nMonday into the middle of next week the ridge flattens some and \nalong with higher PWAT values returning to the area per ensemble \nmean/averages. This should bring a return to some chance for \nrainfall. Still could see some fire weather concerns yet Monday, but \nthese decrease Tuesday into Wednesday as a cold front dives \nsoutheast through the area cooling high temperatures back and \nhumidity on the rise.",
